# private_names.txt — names that must never reach a public-bound file.
#
#   cp system/config/private_names.txt.template system/config/private_names.txt
#
# One name per line. Blank lines and # comments ignored. Matched case-INsensitively
# on word boundaries by scripts/sanitize.sh, which runs as your pre-commit hook.
# (Names leak lowercased more often than capitalised — in filenames, branch names
# and slugs. A case-sensitive check reports "clean" over exactly those.)
#
# One list serves every repo you keep. For repos outside this workshop:
#   ./scripts/configure_workshop_git.sh /path/to/other-repo
#
# The copy you create is gitignored and never leaves your machine. This template
# ships empty on purpose: a framework that shipped real names in order to guard
# them would publish the thing it was guarding.
#
# Who belongs here: partners, children, family, friends, colleagues, guest
# practitioners — anyone real who appears in your practice and did not agree to
# appear in your repository. Add surnames and handles too, not just first names;
# a first name plus a public fork list is often enough to identify someone.
#
# In public-bound writing, use a role instead: "the Mage's partner", "a guest
# practitioner", "a friend". Private paths (desk/, floor/, box/) are not scanned
# — write freely there. This rule guards what is bound for daylight.
#
# Example:
#   Alex
#   Alexandra
#   Okonkwo
#   somehandle
